1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,152
Everything runs on one laptop: what does production need instead?

2
00:00:04,202 --> 00:00:11,736
Kubernetes runs each deployable as replicas behind a Service: the app three times, each adapter twice.

3
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:20,688
Every image is the same Gradle-built jar on the plain JRE, as a user without root rights; the limits and JVM options come from the cluster.

4
00:00:20,738 --> 00:00:25,920
Spring Boot's own probes tell the cluster when a pod is alive, and when it takes traffic.

5
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:34,224
A rolling update starts a new pod before it stops an old one, and a short pause before shutdown lets the Service stop sending requests first.

6
00:00:34,274 --> 00:00:40,344
The app then finishes what it is doing, for up to sixty seconds, longer than the slowest scheme call.

7
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:47,112
Addresses live in a ConfigMap; secrets come from a Secret the bank creates from Vault, never from git.

8
00:00:47,162 --> 00:00:57,696
Three replicas run every scheduled job three times, and that is safe: skip locked, one workflow per payment, ShedLock, and Kafka shares its partitions between them.

9
00:00:57,746 --> 00:01:04,728
An autoscaler adds replicas when the CPU is busy, and a disruption budget never lets a drain take more than one.

10
00:01:04,778 --> 00:01:11,184
Deleting a pod in the middle of payments loses nothing: the others take the traffic, and a new one starts.

11
00:01:11,234 --> 00:01:18,600
On a laptop, the same manifests run in a one-node k3s, with Services that point at the compose stack.

12
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:23,016
Next, the last step: moving the card vault into its own service.
